#!/usr/bin/env bash
# refresh-hyprtasking — EXAMPLE prompt: keep a local Hyprland plugin fork
# (on a personal feature branch) in sync with upstream bug fixes AND in sync
# with the running Hyprland's ABI. Replace the repo path / plugin name /
# branch name with your own; this is illustrative.
#
# Cron-safe contract:
#   • Idempotent — fast no-op when everything is current
#   • Cherry-picks (never rebases) to preserve the fork's divergence from
#     the upstream feature set
#   • On any cherry-pick conflict, aborts cleanly and reports — does not try
#     to resolve
#   • Never touches the upstream remote, never force-pushes
#
# Usage: ../run refresh-hyprtasking

exec claude -p \
'Refresh the example plugin at ~/repos/<your-plugin>, keeping the local fork branch <your-branch> alive.

Decision logic — only do work when needed:

(A) Detect if anything is wrong:
  1. `hyprctl plugin list` — is "<your-plugin>" currently loaded?
  2. Compare mtimes: is build/lib<your-plugin>.so older than /usr/include/hyprland/src/version.h? (older .so → ABI may have shifted, needs rebuild)
  3. cd to the repo, `git fetch origin`. Run `git log --no-merges origin/main..<your-branch>` and `git log --no-merges <your-branch>..origin/main`. Are there new upstream content commits that we have not yet cherry-picked?

(B) If plugin loaded AND .so newer than headers AND no new upstream commits → report "up to date, no action" and exit. This is the common case.

(C) Otherwise, do the minimum work needed:
  1. If new upstream content commits exist: cherry-pick them in chronological order. SKIP merge commits (subject starts with "Merge pull request"). If any cherry-pick reports a conflict, immediately run `git cherry-pick --abort` and STOP — report the conflicting commit(s) and exit non-zero. Never try to resolve conflicts autonomously; that needs a human.
  2. Rebuild: `rm -rf build && meson setup build --buildtype=release && ninja -C build`. If the build fails, STOP and report compiler errors.
  3. Reload: `hyprctl plugin load /home/user/repos/<your-plugin>/build/lib<your-plugin>.so`.
  4. Verify: `hyprctl plugin list` shows "<your-plugin>".

Final summary (one concise paragraph): which actions ran, hashes of any commits cherry-picked, build outcome, load outcome. If nothing was needed, say so plainly.' \
  --allowed-tools 'Bash(cd *) Bash(git *) Bash(meson *) Bash(ninja *) Bash(rm *) Bash(hyprctl *) Bash(stat *) Bash(ls *) Bash(test *) Bash([ *) Read Glob Grep'
